\end{table}

\section{Cross-Judge Validation}
\label{app:cross_judge}

To assess whether violation judgments depend on the specific LLM judge, we re-evaluate all 2,997 ambiguous-condition episodes using three independent judge families: GPT-4.1 (original), Claude Sonnet~4.6, and Gemini~2.5~Pro. Each judge uses the same rubric and prompt template; only the underlying model differs.

\paragraph{Inter-judge agreement.} Fleiss' $\kappa$ across all three judges is 0.717 (substantial agreement). Pairwise Cohen's $\kappa$: GPT--Claude $= 0.776$, GPT--Gemini $= 0.696$, Claude--Gemini $= 0.685$. Gemini is systematically harsher (mean $+$8.8\,pp across all models), while GPT and Claude produce nearly identical rates (mean $|\Delta| = 1.6$\,pp).

\paragraph{Model ranking concordance.} All three judges agree on ranking extremes: Claude Sonnet~4.6 is least vulnerable (rank~5 in all three) and DeepSeek-V3 ranks 1--2 in all three. Pairwise Spearman $\rho$ for model rankings: GPT--Claude $= 0.70$, GPT--Gemini $= 0.80$, Claude--Gemini $= 0.70$ (all $p < 0.20$; with only five models, power is inherently limited).

\paragraph{Type ranking concordance.} Pairwise Spearman $\rho$ for type rankings: GPT--Claude $= 0.71$ ($p = 0.11$), GPT--Gemini $= 0.77$ ($p = 0.07$), Claude--Gemini $= 0.89$ ($p = 0.019$). All three judges rank incompleteness highest ($\geq$46.8\%) and coreferential or conditional precedence lowest. Authorization scope shows the largest cross-judge variance (41.6\%--65.6\%), consistent with its low per-type $\kappa$ (0.385; Table~\ref{tab:per_type_agreement}).

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. Overall awareness is 4.6\%, clustering by model: GPT-4.1 and Claude show 10--11\% while Qwen3 and DeepSeek exhibit zero across 278 episodes ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}).

We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}---extending the ambiguity collapse phenomenon documented in static NLP tasks \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} to agentic policy execution, where it produces irreversible safety violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. A prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.3\,pp, n.s.), reinforcing policy-design over prompt-level fixes.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, conditional OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity (\S\ref{sec:method}). A Type III sum-of-squares decomposition\footnote{Type III SS avoids ordering dependence in our unbalanced design; logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant results (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).} partitions variance as: clause identity 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), model 1.0\%, condition 15.8\%, residual 60.8\% (of which $\sim$15\% is stochastic and $\sim$46\% systematic, estimated from an OpenAI-family ICC replication; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). A permutation test confirms that clause wording---not type membership---drives this variance ($p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}). The incompleteness effect ($+$49.2\,pp) is an upper bound that includes information addition; controlling for token count---a coarse proxy for information content---changes no type's predicted effect by more than 0.1\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). The confound-free range is $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$36.0\,pp (authorization scope); excluding incompleteness entirely, the pooled effect is $+$31.0\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$).

Replication on two additional models confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); SOPBench~\citep{li_sopbench_2025} confirms directional consistency ($+12.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy predicts failure mechanisms substantially more strongly than violation rate (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$; the raw NMI ratio is inflated by target cardinality asymmetry; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

\subsection{Type Partial Ordering (C2)}

